  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "c2p1-pairs",
      "source_numbers": [
        "138"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "multiple",
        "stem": "哪些有序数对是方程 $x^2-3xy+y^2=31$ 的解？",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c2p1-pairs-option-1",
            "text": "$(3+2\\sqrt2,3-2\\sqrt2)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-pairs-option-2",
            "text": "$(-3,-11)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-pairs-option-3",
            "text": "$(2,3)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-pairs-option-4",
            "text": "$(3,-2)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-pairs-option-5",
            "text": "$(3,11)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-pairs-option-6",
            "text": "$(-2,3)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Judge the entire supplied collection by one equation, including the radical pair.",
        "source_solution": "Substitution gives 31,31,31,31,-5,31 in written order. For the radical pair x+y=6,xy=1, so (x+y)^2-5xy=31.",
        "unit_reason": "One shared membership predicate calls for selecting all qualifying pairs, not six binary items.",
        "concept_blocks": [],
        "limitations": [],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "c2p1-pairs-option-2",
          "c2p1-pairs-option-5",
          "c2p1-pairs-option-4",
          "c2p1-pairs-option-6",
          "c2p1-pairs-option-1"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-pairs-option-2",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "9-3(33)+121=31.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-pairs-option-5",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "9-3(33)+121=31.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-pairs-option-4",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "9-3(-6)+4=31.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-pairs-option-6",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "4-3(-6)+9=31.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-pairs-option-3",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Its actual value is 4-18+9=-5. Replacing subtraction of 3xy by addition gives 4+18+9=31 and falsely accepts it.",
            "misconception": "Evaluate -3xy as +3xy for positive x,y."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-pairs-option-1",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "xy=9-8=1 and x+y=6, giving 36-5=31.",
            "misconception": null
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2p1-counts",
      "source_numbers": [
        "139"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "在实数范围内，哪张表正确列出了每个方程的有序数对解的个数？\n\n$E_1:(x^2-7)(y^2+4)=0$；$E_2:(x-2y)(x^2+1)=0$；$E_3:(x^2-9)(y^2-4)=0$；$E_4:(x^2-9)^2+(y^2-4)^2=0$；$E_5:(x-y)(x^2+y^2)=0$；$E_6:(x-y)^2+(y-1)^2=0$。",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c2p1-counts-option-1",
            "text": "| 方程 | $E_1$ | $E_2$ | $E_3$ | $E_4$ | $E_5$ | $E_6$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 解的个数 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 4 | 4 | 无限多 | 1 |",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-counts-option-2",
            "text": "| 方程 | $E_1$ | $E_2$ | $E_3$ | $E_4$ | $E_5$ | $E_6$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 解的个数 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 无限多 |",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-counts-option-3",
            "text": "| 方程 | $E_1$ | $E_2$ | $E_3$ | $E_4$ | $E_5$ | $E_6$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 解的个数 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 无限多 | 4 | 无限多 | 1 |",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Distinguish zero products from sums of nonnegative squares and count real ordered pairs.",
        "source_solution": "E1 gives x=+-sqrt7,y arbitrary; E2 gives x=2y; E3 is x=+-3 OR y=+-2, four lines; E4 requires x=+-3 AND y=+-2, four pairs; E5 is x=y with origin already included; E6 requires x=y=1.",
        "unit_reason": "A complete count table preserves the cross-equation distinction between union and intersection without per-cell clicks.",
        "concept_blocks": [],
        "limitations": [
          "NOT_FULL_COVERAGE: selection does not demonstrate independently deriving the six solution loci."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "c2p1-counts-option-3"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-counts-option-3",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Positive factors eliminate impossible branches; product-zero is OR, squared-sum-zero is AND. Counts are infinite,infinite,infinite,4,infinite,1.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-counts-option-1",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Requiring both x^2-9=0 and y^2-4=0 in E3 produces four pairs instead of four entire lines; other counts remain unchanged.",
            "misconception": "Replace the OR of a zero product by AND."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-counts-option-2",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Apply the zero-product OR rule incorrectly to E4 and E6: either squared term zero gives whole lines, hence infinite for both.",
            "misconception": "A sum of squares zero is misread as requiring only one summand zero."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs",
      "source_numbers": [
        "140"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "multiple",
        "stem": "设 $x,y$ 为实数。哪些等式及下界成立，因而能证明相应表达式不可能等于零？",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-1",
            "text": "$x^2+8x+y^2+17=(x+4)^2+y^2+13\\ge13$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-2",
            "text": "$x^2+8x+y^2+17=(x+4)^2+y^2+1\\ge1$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-3",
            "text": "$x^2-10x+y^2+100=(x-5)^2+y^2+75\\ge75$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-4",
            "text": "$x^2-10x+y^2+100=(x-10)^2+y^2\\ge100$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-5",
            "text": "$x^2-2xy+2y^2+4y+7=(x-y)^2+(y+2)^2+3\\ge3$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-6",
            "text": "$x^2-2xy+y^2+8=(x-y)^2+8\\ge8$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Recognize valid completed-square proofs of nonexistence for all four expressions.",
        "source_solution": "The exact square decompositions have positive residual constants 8,1,75,3 respectively, hence no expression can vanish for real x,y.",
        "unit_reason": "All four share one proof criterion: identity plus strictly positive global lower bound. One multiple item evaluates that criterion across the collection.",
        "concept_blocks": [],
        "limitations": [
          "NOT_FULL_COVERAGE: recognizing a supplied proof does not assess producing the square completion or writing a proof."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-2",
          "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-5",
          "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-3",
          "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-6"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-2",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Expanding (x+4)^2 adds 16, leaving 17-16=1; both squares are nonnegative.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-5",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Expanding the squares gives x^2-2xy+2y^2+4y+4, leaving 3.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-3",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Half the coefficient -10 is -5; square completion adds 25 and leaves 75.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-6",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "x^2-2xy+y^2=(x-y)^2; its minimum is zero, so the minimum left side is 8.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-1",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Subtracting 4 instead of 4^2 from 17 leaves 13; expansion then gives constant 29, not 17.",
            "misconception": "Subtract the shift rather than its square during completion."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-positive-proofs-option-4",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Using the full coefficient -10 as shift produces -20x on expansion. Retaining the original constant 100 as a lower bound also ignores that (x-10)^2+y^2 has minimum zero.",
            "misconception": "Use the full linear coefficient for the shift and keep the old constant as the minimum."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2p1-unique-proofs",
      "source_numbers": [
        "141"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "multiple",
        "stem": "在实数范围内，哪些论证正确给出了相应方程的唯一解？",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-1",
            "text": "$2x^2-6xy+9y^2-10x+25=0$ 等价于 $(x-3y)^2+(x-5)^2=0$，故唯一解是 $(5,15)$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-2",
            "text": "$x^2+6x+y^2-10y=-34$ 等价于 $(x+3)^2+(y-5)^2=0$，故唯一解是 $(-3,5)$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-3",
            "text": "$x^2+6x+y^2-10y=-34$ 等价于 $(x+3)^2+(y-5)^2=0$，故唯一解是 $(3,-5)$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-4",
            "text": "$2x^2-6xy+9y^2-10x+25=0$ 等价于 $(x-3y)^2+(x-5)^2=0$，故唯一解是 $(5,\\frac53)$。",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Prove uniqueness by forcing both completed squares to vanish, then solve the resulting linear constraints.",
        "source_solution": "The first becomes (x+3)^2+(y-5)^2=0, yielding (-3,5). The second is (x-3y)^2+(x-5)^2=0, yielding x=5,y=5/3. Both equivalences give existence and uniqueness.",
        "unit_reason": "The shared sum-of-squares uniqueness criterion makes a single proof-recognition collection meaningful.",
        "concept_blocks": [],
        "limitations": [
          "NOT_FULL_COVERAGE: independent proof composition and finding the decomposition are not assessed."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-2",
          "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-4"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-2",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Each square must be zero: x+3=0,y-5=0. The one pair satisfies the original equation.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-1",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "From x=5 and x=3y, reversing the correspondence to y=3x yields y=15 instead of 5/3.",
            "misconception": "Solve x=3y as y=3x."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-4",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "x-5=0 and x-3y=0 imply x=5,y=5/3; both squares vanish only there.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c2p1-unique-proofs-option-3",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Read the shifts +3 and -5 as coordinates rather than setting x+3 and y-5 to zero, giving (3,-5).",
            "misconception": "Keep the written shift signs instead of solving the zero factors."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2p1-finite-examples",
      "source_numbers": [
